    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nVolkswagen ID.4-Replacing ID. Tiguan Starts Production at Emden Plant in Germany\n81 photos\nPhoto: Volkswagen\nEurope's largest automaker by global sales has recently started assembling the ID. Tiguan. Hailing from the Emden factory in northern Germany, the zero-emission model doesn't replace only the ID.4 crossover. For model year 2027, the ID.5 isn't coming back due to sluggish demand across European markets.\nVolkswagen says that ID. Tiguan deliveries will begin in the earliest part of 2027. This crossover couldn't have arrived at a more awkward moment for the Emden complex, which faces an uncertain future under the Wolfsburg-based manufacturer's latest restructuring plan.\nThe ID. Tiguan will be launching in three powertrain configurations, split between rear-wheel drive and 4MOTION dual-motor AWD. Production output should increase gradually over the coming weeks. Adopting more conventional styling than the ID.4, the newcomer benefits from a more upright front end, more pronounced surfaces, and pull-type handles instead of the flush nonsense from before.\nVolkswagen has also addressed the problem regarding physical controls, which now replace some of the previous touch-sensitive arrangements. Its dashboard combines a 10-inch digital instrument cluster with a 15-inch central touchscreen, and there's also an optional 12-inch passenger display that needs highlighting.\nGifted with a small front storage compartment, the ID. Tiguan is listed with 534 liters (18.8 cubic feet) of storage capacity behind the rear seats. Folding them flat into the floor gets you 1,577 liters, namely 55.7 cubic feet where metric speak is frowned upon. The base rear-drive specification uses a 58-kWh battery of the LFP variety and a drive unit that outputs 187 horsepower.\nPhoto: Volkswagen The most you can ask of it with a full charge is 450 kilometers (280 miles) in the WLTP test cycle, while 100 kilometers per hour (62 miles per hour) is doable in 8.5 seconds. Prospective customers who need more driving range are presented with a 77-kWh pack that employs a nickel-manganese-cobalt chemistry. This battery can be had with either rear- or all-wheel drive.\nRWD means 282 horsepower and no more than 600 kilometers (373 miles), with 0-100 estimated at 6.7 seconds. Opting for 4MOTION means 295 horsepower and 560 kilometers (348 miles), with Volkswagen also quoting a 6.1-second sprint to 100 clicks for the European-spec model. Charging-wise, the LFP and NMC batteries can take up to 105 and 165 kilowatts, respectively.\nThat's 26 or 29 minutes from 10 to 80 percent on direct current, with both versions supporting 11 kilowatts on AC. Available to configure from 42,695 euros in Germany, the ID. Tiguan tows either 1,800 kilograms (3,968 pounds) with the lesser pack or 2,000 kilograms (4,409 pounds) with the larger one.\nVolkswagen's Emden facility used to build the Beetle in the 1960s, after which the Passat became its defining product. In preparation for the ID.4, the plant's transition toward electric began in 2020 as part of an investment totaling more than 1 billion euros. More than 500,000 electric vehicles have been produced there since 2022, and the final combustion vehicle left Emden in 2024.\nUnder the automaker's Future Plan 2030 strategy, Volkswagen cannot guarantee future production allocations for Emden and three additional plants between 2031 and 2034. The Wolfsburg-based colossus has to reduce excess manufacturing capacity in Europe by 500,000 vehicles fast, and more details about Emden's future will be revealed by the end of June 2027.\nAs it stands, the ID. Tiguan needs to generate sufficient production volume to support a plant whose future is still at play. Whether the Emden complex will be allocated new models in the 2030s, that will partly depend on the commercial success of the compact-sized electric crossover.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 Volkswagen ID. Tiguan Volkswagen ID. Tiguan Volkswagen production EV\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 33-Year-Old Drives Around With a Bugatti Chiron in Pokémon-Inspired Livery, Still Flies Economy",
